    <!-- First run -->
    <string name="fr_step1_title">1 · Plug in the LED controller</string>
    <string name="fr_step1_text">Connect the controller\'s USB cable to the TV. When Android asks, allow TV Ambient to use it.</string>
    <string name="fr_step2_title">2 · Turn on USB debugging</string>
    <string name="fr_dev_off">Developer options are off. Open About, find Build (or Android TV OS build) and press OK on it seven times, until the TV says you\'re a developer. This screen comes back by itself.\n\nWithout USB debugging, Screen mode uses screen recording, which costs more.</string>
    <string name="fr_dev_on">Developer options are on. Open them and turn on USB debugging (and Network debugging, if your TV lists it). This screen comes back by itself.\n\nWithout USB debugging, Screen mode uses screen recording, which costs more.</string>
    <string name="fr_adb_on">USB debugging is on. When the TV asks \"Allow USB debugging?\", tick Always allow and press OK.</string>
    <string name="fr_dev_off_status">Developer options: off</string>
    <string name="fr_dev_on_status">Developer options: on · USB debugging: off</string>
    <string name="fr_open_about">Open About</string>
    <string name="fr_open_dev">Open Developer options</string>
    <string name="fr_step3_title">3 · Set up the strip</string>
    <string name="fr_step3_text">Count the LEDs on each side and where LED 1 is. The next screen draws them round the edge so you can match them to the wall.</string>
